#  Answers - Visualiztion  B

    > What software did you use to create your data visualization?

    Excel (Microsoft, Windows 11)

    > Who is your intended audience? 
    
    My visualization aims to inform manergers and policy makers in the Toronto's Shelters and Support Servises (TSSS), as well as social workers,
    shelters' manergers, and academics. In addition, it aims to highlit differences in occupancy rates acctoss the five shelter sectors: women,
    man, youth, mixed adults, and families, so that each group can advocacte specifically for its needs.  

    > What information or message are you trying to convey with your visualization? 
    
    The purpose of this visualization is to reflect the need to build more emergency shelters. As in visualization A, this visualization underscores
    the urgent need to build more shelters by calculating the annual average of occupancy rates across all sectors of shelter systems (ranging between
    93.46 for youth to 97.83 for women). In addition, this visualization is based on the results of a one-way annova test table, reflecting the differences
    between these sectors (which were found to be significant, p<0.05). For example, youth, men, and mixed adult shelters have lower annual occupancy
    rate averages and higher standard deviations compared to the women and families shelters. This could be caused by a lack of facilities and beds for the women's and family 
    sectors compared to the men and young people, or by men and young people underutilizing the services of the wardens 
    (possibly due to physical conditions, safety, staff, etc.).

    > What aspects of design did you consider when making your visualization? How did you apply them? With what elements of your plots? 
    
    Firstly, I created two bar charts for each sector, showing the average annual occupancy rate and its standard deviation.
    I use two different colours, ensuring that one is darker than the other (see accessibility considerations). 
    I also placed the legend at the bottom  of the figure so it would not interrupt the interpretability of the visualization. 
    Additionally, I used the option of "data tags" so that each bar is topped with its value, making the reading of the visualization 
    easier (particularly given that the differences across averages are not so significant and without numbers are hard to notice). 
    In this regard, I also increased the figure size to make interpretation easier. 


    > How did you ensure that your data visualizations are reproducible? If the tool you used to make your data visualization is not reproducible, how will this impact your data visualization? 
    
    I employed several strategies to ensure reproducibility. First, I downloaded and saved the original dataset as a CSV file on my local machine (see the attached file). 
    Secondly, I used a separate Excel sheet to extract and construct the variables (daily occupancy (dependent) rate factored by sector [see attached as XLS]). 
    Doing so, I preserved the original dataset (reusability). Thirdly, I commented on my Excel sheet and provided a detailed description of the dataset and 
    operations done on this dataset (attached also as a pdf). Lastly, I included the dataset's reference (URL) in the dataset description.

    > How did you ensure that your data visualization is accessible?  
    
    I employed several methods to make my visualization accessible. Firstly, I used different colours for the bar and line plots. The colours are also different
    from one another in their darkness level (orange versus dark blue), making the visualization accessible for people with colour vision deficiencies. 
    Secondly, I used an accessible font family ('Arial Black') for the title, axes labels, axes ticks, and legend. Finally, I refrained from using acronyms
    (e.g. standard deviations) to ensure accessibility for people unfamiliar with statistical jargon. 

    > Who are the individuals and communities who might be impacted by your visualization?  
    
    My visualization will impact both shelter residents and workers. It aims to alleviate the overcrowding in Toronto's shelter system
    by debunking the idea that the overoccupancy is only a winter-related phenomenon. This, I hope, will encourage the municipality to build
    more shelters, and long-term housing solutions. 


    > How did you choose which features of your chosen dataset to include or exclude from your visualization? 
    
    My motivation was to show that monthly average occupancy rates in both bed and room shrelters (the only two occupancy rates calculated in
    this dataset) are nearing 100% in all months of the year and across sectors. The dataset includes only daily occupancy rate calculations.
    Additionally, I wanted to draw stakeholders' attention to the overall differences in occupancy rate averages and variability across sectors, 
    and motivate these stakeholders to understand the causes of these differences better. 


